{"task_id": "HumanEval/58", "solution": "def common(l1: list, l2: list):\n    \"\"\"Return sorted unique common elements for two lists.\n    >>> common([1, 4, 3, 34, 653, 2, 5], [5, 7, 1, 5, 9, 653, 121])\n    [1, 5, 653]\n    >>> common([5, 3, 2, 8], [3, 2])\n    [2, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        # Preferred algorithm: convert to sets and compute intersection.\n        # This will raise TypeError if elements are unhashable.\n        set_l1 = set(l1)\n        set_l2 = set(l2)\n        common_elements = list(set_l1.intersection(set_l2))\n    except TypeError:\n        # Fallback for unhashable elements: iterate l1 and check membership in l2.\n        # A list is used to track seen common items for uniqueness if they are unhashable.\n        common_elements = []\n        seen_in_common = []\n        for item_l1 in l1:\n            if item_l1 in l2:\n                if item_l1 not in seen_in_common:\n                    seen_in_common.append(item_l1)\n        common_elements = seen_in_common\n\n    try:\n        # Attempt to sort the resulting list of common elements.\n        # This will raise TypeError if elements are not comparable.\n        common_elements.sort()\n    except TypeError:\n        # If elements are not comparable, return the unique common elements unsorted.\n        pass\n\n    return common_elements"}
