{"task_id": "HumanEval/76", "solution": "def is_simple_power(x, n):\n    \"\"\"Return True if there exists a non-negative integer k such that n**k == x.\n\n    Args:\n        x (int): value to test\n        n (int): base\n\n    Returns:\n        bool\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(x, int) or not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"x and n must be integers\")\n\n    # 1 is n**0 for any integer n (including n==0 by Python convention)\n    if x == 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Handle small/base edge cases\n    if n == 0:\n        # 0**k == 0 for k >= 1 (and Python defines 0**0 == 1 which is handled above)\n        return x == 0\n    if n == 1:\n        # 1**k == 1 only (x==1 handled above)\n        return False\n    if n == -1:\n        # (-1)**k is either 1 or -1\n        return x in (1, -1)\n\n    if x == 0:\n        # Non-zero base can never produce 0\n        return False\n\n    # If base positive, negative x cannot be produced.\n    if n > 0 and x < 0:\n        return False\n\n    current = x\n    # Repeatedly divide by n while divisible; if we reach 1 or n, it's a match.\n    while current != 1 and current != n:\n        if current % n != 0:\n            return False\n        current //= n\n\n    return current == 1 or current == n"}
